Running LTSP clients entirely from RAM after boot (toram)
============================================================

Goal: a thin client pulls its root image over NBD as usual, but once
booted it no longer depends on the network or the boot server at all -
if the server, the switch, or the client's NIC goes down afterward, the
client keeps running unaffected. Useful for reducing server/network
load on large deployments, and for resilience during network or
server maintenance.

This is implemented as a new initramfs-tools local-bottom script,
client/Debian/share/initramfs-tools/scripts/local-bottom/nbd_toram
(shipped in the ltsp-client-core package), modeled on the same "toram"
kernel parameter name and idea used by Debian Live's live-boot.


How it works
----------------
The client boots and connects to the NBD root image exactly as before
(initramfs-tools' stock nbd support plus this project's existing
local-top/nbd_ltsp tweaks - unchanged). Once the root filesystem has
been mounted read-only from /dev/nbd0 (still inside the initramfs,
before switch_root hands off to the real init), the new nbd_toram
script:

  1. Reads the raw NBD device size (blockdev --getsize64).
  2. Mounts a tmpfs at /run/toram sized to the image plus 64 MB of
     headroom for tmpfs/loop overhead.
  3. dd's the entire raw block device into /run/toram/root.img. This
     copies the compressed squashfs image as-is (not the decompressed
     tree), so the RAM cost is the .img file size, not the uncompressed
     content size.
  4. Moves the existing root mount aside, loop-mounts root.img
     read-only in its place, and only then disconnects nbd-client
     (nbd-client -d /dev/nbd0). No process is on the network image
     while any of this happens - the switch to the RAM copy is atomic
     from the rest of the boot process's point of view.
  5. If anything above fails (not enough RAM for the tmpfs, dd error,
     mount failure), the script logs it and leaves the original
     NBD-backed root exactly as it was - a client with too little RAM
     or a bad image never fails to boot because of this feature, it
     just boots as a normal network-dependent thin client instead.

Since squashfs is already read-only and the image itself doesn't
change while a client is running, there's nothing to write back or
synchronize - this is a pure read-only cache-to-RAM, not a live/union
filesystem trick.


Enabling it
---------------
Add "toram" to the client's kernel command line (PXE/pxelinux APPEND
line, GRUB, or your DHCP/PXE menu tool - wherever ROOT=/dev/nbd0 and
the rest of the boot parameters are already set for that client).

This is deliberately a *boot parameter*, not an lts.conf setting: the
decision has to be made inside the initramfs, before the running
system is even far enough along to fetch lts.conf via getltscfg. If
you want it per-client rather than for everyone, use your PXE tool's
per-MAC/per-host config file mechanism to add "toram" only to the
clients you want it for (e.g. ones with enough RAM).


RAM sizing
--------------
Rule of thumb: available RAM should be at least the compressed image
size plus what a normal desktop session plus ldm/X/xfreerdp actually
use at runtime (same headroom you'd want on the same hardware without
toram) - and add a safety margin on top rather than sizing exactly to
the image, since the client also needs headroom for its own runtime
tmpfs usage (/tmp, /var/log, etc., which are unrelated to this feature
and already RAM-backed as before).

Verified live (see below): copying a full 638 MiB real LTSP client
image took 1.2 seconds at ~560 MB/s. Copy time scales with image size
and storage/network throughput on the server side during the initial
NBD pull; once in RAM there's no further network dependency at all.


Live test results
---------------------
Tested end-to-end twice: first with a synthetic 50 MB squashfs image
to validate the mechanism in isolation, then with a real 638 MiB LTSP
client chroot built by ltsp-build-client on Debian 13 (trixie),
including ldm 3.0.0, X.org, and xfreerdp3 - the actual content a real
thin client would boot.

Both times: mounted the image from a real nbd-server via nbd-client,
copied it into a tmpfs, switched the mount over to the RAM copy, then
fully disconnected nbd-client AND killed the nbd-server process
entirely. Confirmed:

  - No client-to-server network connection remained (checked via ss).
  - File content was still fully intact (checksums matched the
    pre-copy originals for both the synthetic payload and the real
    image's /usr/sbin/ldm binary).
  - The RAM-backed image was still mountable and executable: chroot'd
    into it and ran a binary successfully after the server was gone.

This confirms the copy-to-RAM-then-disconnect approach works correctly
against a real, complete LTSP image, not just in theory.

While building and testing this, packaging the client-side scripts for
real (see the ltsp-client-core Debian package) surfaced and fixed
three genuine, pre-existing bugs that would otherwise have broken
image builds on any current kernel:

  - client/Debian/share/initramfs-tools/hooks/ltsp requested two
    kernel modules that don't exist on any current Debian kernel
    ("overlayfs" - the module was renamed to "overlay" years ago - and
    "aufs" - never mainlined). Under this script's "set -e", the very
    first manual_add_modules call for a nonexistent module aborted the
    entire hook, which made update-initramfs fail on every install.
    Fixed by dropping the two dead module names.
  - The same hook also copy_exec's /usr/sbin/nbd-client-proxy into the
    initramfs, but the ltsp-client-core package only shipped the
    compiled nbd-proxy binary, not this second file (a plain shell
    wrapper script living alongside it in client/nbd-proxy/src/). Fixed
    by adding it to the package.
  - ltsp-client-core didn't depend on the nbd-client package at all,
    even though the same hook requires the nbd-client binary to exist
    in the chroot at initramfs-generation time. Fixed by adding
    nbd-client to ltsp-client-core's Depends.

Without these three fixes, no client chroot on a current Debian kernel
could even finish its initramfs regeneration after installing
ltsp-client-core, let alone boot with or without toram.


Building and maintaining thin-client and fat-client images independently
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
A related, separate question: can a "thin" (network-apps-only) image
and a "fat" (local-apps) image be built and kept independently of each
other? Yes - this is already fully supported by the existing tooling,
no new code needed:

  - ltsp-build-client --chroot=NAME names the target chroot directory
    explicitly (defaults to the detected architecture if unset - see
    server/share/ltsp/plugins/ltsp-build-client/common/010-set-chroot).
    So e.g.:
      ltsp-build-client --dist trixie --chroot amd64-thin
      ltsp-build-client --dist trixie --chroot amd64-fat \
                         --fat-client-desktop=task-xfce-desktop
    produces two completely independent chroots under $BASE
    (/opt/ltsp/amd64-thin, /opt/ltsp/amd64-fat by default), each with
    its own package set, its own updates, and no shared state.
  - ltsp-update-image, ltsp-chroot, ltsp-update-kernels etc. all accept
    the same CHROOT name/path as an argument, so each image is updated,
    rebuilt, and imaged independently (amd64-thin.img, amd64-fat.img).
  - Serve both simultaneously: give each its own nbd-server export
    (ltsp-update-image's -f/--config-nbd already generates one
    /etc/nbd-server/conf.d/<chroot>.conf per chroot/image), and point
    each client group at the image it should use via your PXE
    menu/per-host config (or DHCP host declarations), the same way you
    already point different client groups at different lts.conf
    settings.

In short: "thin" vs "fat" today is mostly a runtime distinction
(LTSP_FATCLIENT in lts.conf, auto-detected per client based on
available RAM and installed desktop sessions - see
client/share/ltsp/ltsp_config.d/20fatclients), layered on top of a
single --fat-client-desktop image that has the extra desktop packages
installed. If you'd rather have two genuinely separate, independently
maintained images instead of one shared fat/thin-capable image, the
--chroot mechanism above gives you that directly.
